window.docContents[96].resolve({"/Judgments-and-domains/#Core-C______--Judgments-and-domains":{"contents":"Core C++ has three semantic components. The typing context  maps identifiers to types. The environment  maps identifiers to locations . The store  maps locations to values, and its domain is the set of live locations.\n\nThe notation is the sequent style of Kahn (1987). Hypotheses stand left of , the subject right of it, and the result after ,  or .\n\nEach judgment is an inductive proposition of CoreCpp/Semantics/, with one constructor per rule, and the type checker of CoreCpp/Typing.lean and the evaluator of CoreCpp/Eval.lean are the functions that interpret them, . Every node of a judgment points at both, the relation and the function.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Core C++","header":"2. Judgments and domains","id":"/Judgments-and-domains/#Core-C______--Judgments-and-domains"}});